{
 "content": [
  {
   "attrs": {
    "textAlign": "justify"
   },
   "content": [
    {
     "text": "Although you may have thought you had a pretty good grasp on the notion of counting at the age of three, it turns out that you had to wait until now to learn how to really count. Aren’t you glad you took this class now?!!! But seriously, counting is like the foundation of a house (where the house is all the great things we will do later in this book, such as machine learning). Houses are awesome. Foundations, on the other hand, are pretty much just concrete in a hole. But don’t make a house without a foundation. It won’t turn out well.",
     "type": "text"
    }
   ],
   "type": "paragraph"
  },
  {
   "attrs": {
    "id": "counting-with-steps",
    "level": "2",
    "textAlign": "justify"
   },
   "content": [
    {
     "marks": [
      {
       "type": "bold"
      }
     ],
     "text": "Counting with Steps",
     "type": "text"
    }
   ],
   "type": "heading"
  },
  {
   "content": [
    {
     "attrs": {
      "textAlign": "justify"
     },
     "content": [
      {
       "marks": [
        {
         "type": "bold"
        },
        {
         "type": "italic"
        }
       ],
       "text": "Definition",
       "type": "text"
      },
      {
       "text": ": Step Rule of Counting (aka Product Rule of Counting)",
       "type": "text"
      }
     ],
     "type": "paragraph"
    },
    {
     "attrs": {
      "textAlign": "justify"
     },
     "content": [
      {
       "text": "If an experiment has two steps, where the first step can result in one of ",
       "type": "text"
      },
      {
       "attrs": {
        "showEditor": false,
        "value": "m"
       },
       "type": "math_inline"
      },
      {
       "text": " outcomes and the second step can result in one of ",
       "type": "text"
      },
      {
       "attrs": {
        "showEditor": false,
        "value": "n"
       },
       "type": "math_inline"
      },
      {
       "text": " outcomes regardless of the outcome of the first part, then the total number of outcomes for the experiment is ",
       "type": "text"
      },
      {
       "attrs": {
        "showEditor": false,
        "value": "m⋅n"
       },
       "type": "math_inline"
      },
      {
       "text": ".",
       "type": "text"
      }
     ],
     "type": "paragraph"
    }
   ],
   "type": "borderedBox"
  },
  {
   "attrs": {
    "textAlign": "justify"
   },
   "content": [
    {
     "marks": [
      {
       "attrs": {
        "color": "rgb(33, 37, 41)"
       },
       "type": "textStyle"
      }
     ],
     "text": "Rewritten using set notation, the Step Rule of Counting states that if an experiment with two parts has an outcome from set ",
     "type": "text"
    },
    {
     "attrs": {
      "showEditor": false,
      "value": "A"
     },
     "type": "math_inline"
    },
    {
     "marks": [
      {
       "attrs": {
        "color": "rgb(33, 37, 41)"
       },
       "type": "textStyle"
      }
     ],
     "text": " in the first part, where ",
     "type": "text"
    },
    {
     "attrs": {
      "showEditor": false,
      "value": "|A|=m"
     },
     "type": "math_inline"
    },
    {
     "marks": [
      {
       "attrs": {
        "color": "rgb(33, 37, 41)"
       },
       "type": "textStyle"
      }
     ],
     "text": ", and an outcome from set ",
     "type": "text"
    },
    {
     "attrs": {
      "showEditor": false,
      "value": "B"
     },
     "type": "math_inline"
    },
    {
     "marks": [
      {
       "attrs": {
        "color": "rgb(33, 37, 41)"
       },
       "type": "textStyle"
      }
     ],
     "text": " in the second part (where the number of outcomes in ",
     "type": "text"
    },
    {
     "attrs": {
      "showEditor": false,
      "value": "B"
     },
     "type": "math_inline"
    },
    {
     "marks": [
      {
       "attrs": {
        "color": "rgb(33, 37, 41)"
       },
       "type": "textStyle"
      }
     ],
     "text": " is the same regardless of the outcome of the first part), where ",
     "type": "text"
    },
    {
     "attrs": {
      "showEditor": false,
      "value": "|B|=n"
     },
     "type": "math_inline"
    },
    {
     "marks": [
      {
       "attrs": {
        "color": "rgb(33, 37, 41)"
       },
       "type": "textStyle"
      }
     ],
     "text": ", then the total number of outcomes of the experiment is ",
     "type": "text"
    },
    {
     "attrs": {
      "showEditor": false,
      "value": "|A||B|=m⋅n"
     },
     "type": "math_inline"
    },
    {
     "marks": [
      {
       "attrs": {
        "color": "rgb(33, 37, 41)"
       },
       "type": "textStyle"
      }
     ],
     "text": ".",
     "type": "text"
    }
   ],
   "type": "paragraph"
  },
  {
   "content": [
    {
     "attrs": {
      "textAlign": "justify"
     },
     "content": [
      {
       "marks": [
        {
         "type": "bold"
        },
        {
         "type": "italic"
        }
       ],
       "text": "Simple Example",
       "type": "text"
      },
      {
       "marks": [
        {
         "attrs": {
          "color": "rgb(33, 37, 41)"
         },
         "type": "textStyle"
        }
       ],
       "text": ": Hashing Buckets",
       "type": "text"
      }
     ],
     "type": "paragraph"
    },
    {
     "attrs": {
      "textAlign": "justify"
     },
     "content": [
      {
       "marks": [
        {
         "attrs": {
          "color": "rgb(33, 37, 41)"
         },
         "type": "textStyle"
        }
       ],
       "text": "Consider a hash table with 100 buckets. Two arbitrary strings are independently hashed and added to the table. How many possible ways are there for the strings to be stored in the table? Each string can be hashed to one of 100 buckets. Since the results of hashing the first string do not impact the hash of the second, there are 100 * 100 = 10,000 ways that the two strings may be stored in the hash table.",
       "type": "text"
      },
      {
       "text": " ",
       "type": "text"
      }
     ],
     "type": "paragraph"
    }
   ],
   "type": "purpleBox"
  },
  {
   "attrs": {
    "textAlign": "justify"
   },
   "content": [
    {
     "text": "The step rule scales nicely if there are many parts to an experiment:",
     "type": "text"
    }
   ],
   "type": "paragraph"
  },
  {
   "content": [
    {
     "attrs": {
      "textAlign": "left"
     },
     "content": [
      {
       "marks": [
        {
         "type": "bold"
        },
        {
         "type": "italic"
        }
       ],
       "text": "Definition",
       "type": "text"
      },
      {
       "text": ": Step Rule of Counting with Many Steps",
       "type": "text"
      }
     ],
     "type": "paragraph"
    },
    {
     "attrs": {
      "textAlign": "justify"
     },
     "content": [
      {
       "text": "If an experiment has ",
       "type": "text"
      },
      {
       "attrs": {
        "showEditor": false,
        "value": "k"
       },
       "type": "math_inline"
      },
      {
       "text": " parts, where the first step has ",
       "type": "text"
      },
      {
       "attrs": {
        "showEditor": false,
        "value": "n_1"
       },
       "type": "math_inline"
      },
      {
       "text": " outcomes and the ",
       "type": "text"
      },
      {
       "attrs": {
        "showEditor": false,
        "value": "i"
       },
       "type": "math_inline"
      },
      {
       "text": "th step has ",
       "type": "text"
      },
      {
       "attrs": {
        "showEditor": false,
        "value": "n_i"
       },
       "type": "math_inline"
      },
      {
       "text": " outcomes (regardless of the result of any earlier steps), then the total number of outcomes of the experiment is:",
       "type": "text"
      }
     ],
     "type": "paragraph"
    },
    {
     "attrs": {
      "rawTex": "\\begin{aligned}\n\\text{Number of Outcomes} = \\prod_{i=1}^k n_i\n\\end{aligned}"
     },
     "type": "block-tex"
    }
   ],
   "type": "borderedBox"
  },
  {
   "attrs": {
    "textAlign": "justify"
   },
   "content": [
    {
     "marks": [
      {
       "attrs": {
        "class": null,
        "href": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Peter_Norvig",
        "rel": "noopener noreferrer nofollow",
        "target": "_blank"
       },
       "type": "link"
      }
     ],
     "text": "Peter Norvig",
     "type": "text"
    },
    {
     "text": ", the author of the canonical textbook \"Artificial Intelligence\", made the following compelling point on why computer scientists need to know how to count. To start, let's set a baseline for a really big number: The number of atoms in the observable universe, often estimated to be around 10 to the 80th power (",
     "type": "text"
    },
    {
     "attrs": {
      "showEditor": false,
      "value": "10^{80}"
     },
     "type": "math_inline"
    },
    {
     "text": "). There certainly are a lot of atoms in the universe. As a leading expert said,",
     "type": "text"
    }
   ],
   "type": "paragraph"
  },
  {
   "content": [
    {
     "attrs": {
      "textAlign": "justify"
     },
     "content": [
      {
       "text": "“Space is big. Really big. You just won’t believe how vastly, hugely, mind-bogglingly big it is. I mean, you may think it’s a long way down the road to the chemist, but that’s just peanuts to space.” - Douglas Adams",
       "type": "text"
      }
     ],
     "type": "paragraph"
    }
   ],
   "type": "blockquote"
  },
  {
   "attrs": {
    "textAlign": "justify"
   },
   "content": [
    {
     "text": "This number is often used to demonstrate tasks that computers will never be able to solve. Problems can quickly grow to an absurd size, and we can understand why using the Step Rule of Counting.",
     "type": "text"
    }
   ],
   "type": "paragraph"
  },
  {
   "attrs": {
    "textAlign": "justify"
   },
   "content": [
    {
     "text": "There is an art project to display every possible picture. Surely that would take a long time, because there must be many possible pictures. But how many? We will assume the color model known as ",
     "type": "text"
    },
    {
     "marks": [
      {
       "attrs": {
        "class": null,
        "href": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Color_depth#True_color_(24-bit)",
        "rel": "noopener noreferrer nofollow",
        "target": "_blank"
       },
       "type": "link"
      }
     ],
     "text": "True Color",
     "type": "text"
    },
    {
     "text": ", in which each ",
     "type": "text"
    },
    {
     "marks": [
      {
       "attrs": {
        "class": null,
        "href": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pixel",
        "rel": "noopener noreferrer nofollow",
        "target": "_blank"
       },
       "type": "link"
      }
     ],
     "text": "pixel",
     "type": "text"
    },
    {
     "text": " can be one of ",
     "type": "text"
    },
    {
     "attrs": {
      "showEditor": false,
      "value": "2^{24}"
     },
     "type": "math_inline"
    },
    {
     "text": " ≈ 17 million distinct colors.",
     "type": "text"
    }
   ],
   "type": "paragraph"
  },
  {
   "attrs": {
    "textAlign": "justify"
   },
   "content": [
    {
     "text": "How many distinct pictures can you generate from (a) a smartphone camera shown with 12 million pixels, (b) a grid with 300 pixels, and (c) a grid with just 12 pixels?",
     "type": "text"
    }
   ],
   "type": "paragraph"
  },
  {
   "attrs": {
    "align": "center",
    "id": "7eb92d05-0b62-443d-ab93-424c28bc4e7d",
    "naturalHeight": "342",
    "naturalWidth": "1117",
    "src": "https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/probabilityforcs.firebasestorage.app/o/uploads%2Fimages%2F7eb92d05-0b62-443d-ab93-424c28bc4e7d?alt=media&token=88c0f3cf-1960-4f7d-ba85-b6ba0a87257b",
    "widthPct": "70"
   },
   "type": "kindImage"
  },
  {
   "attrs": {
    "textAlign": "justify"
   },
   "content": [
    {
     "marks": [
      {
       "attrs": {
        "color": "rgb(33, 37, 41)"
       },
       "type": "textStyle"
      }
     ],
     "text": "Answer: We can use the step rule of counting. An image can be created one pixel at a time, step by step. Each time we choose a pixel you can select its color out of 17 million choices. An array of ",
     "type": "text"
    },
    {
     "text": "n",
     "type": "text"
    },
    {
     "marks": [
      {
       "attrs": {
        "color": "rgb(33, 37, 41)"
       },
       "type": "textStyle"
      }
     ],
     "text": " pixels produces (17 million)",
     "type": "text"
    },
    {
     "attrs": {
      "showEditor": false,
      "value": "^n"
     },
     "type": "math_inline"
    },
    {
     "marks": [
      {
       "attrs": {
        "color": "rgb(33, 37, 41)"
       },
       "type": "textStyle"
      }
     ],
     "text": " different pictures. (17 million)",
     "type": "text"
    },
    {
     "attrs": {
      "showEditor": false,
      "value": "^{12}"
     },
     "type": "math_inline"
    },
    {
     "marks": [
      {
       "attrs": {
        "color": "rgb(33, 37, 41)"
       },
       "type": "textStyle"
      }
     ],
     "text": " ≈ ",
     "type": "text"
    },
    {
     "attrs": {
      "showEditor": false,
      "value": "10^{86}"
     },
     "type": "math_inline"
    },
    {
     "marks": [
      {
       "attrs": {
        "color": "rgb(33, 37, 41)"
       },
       "type": "textStyle"
      }
     ],
     "text": ", so the tiny 12-pixel grid produces a million times more pictures than the number of atoms in the universe! How about the 300 pixel array? It can produce ",
     "type": "text"
    },
    {
     "attrs": {
      "showEditor": false,
      "value": "10^{2167}"
     },
     "type": "math_inline"
    },
    {
     "marks": [
      {
       "attrs": {
        "color": "rgb(33, 37, 41)"
       },
       "type": "textStyle"
      }
     ],
     "text": " pictures. You may think the number of atoms in the universe is big, but that’s just peanuts to the number of pictures in a 300-pixel array. And 12M pixels? ",
     "type": "text"
    },
    {
     "attrs": {
      "showEditor": false,
      "value": "10^{86696638}"
     },
     "type": "math_inline"
    },
    {
     "marks": [
      {
       "attrs": {
        "color": "rgb(33, 37, 41)"
       },
       "type": "textStyle"
      }
     ],
     "text": " pictures.",
     "type": "text"
    }
   ],
   "type": "paragraph"
  },
  {
   "content": [
    {
     "attrs": {
      "textAlign": "justify"
     },
     "content": [
      {
       "marks": [
        {
         "type": "bold"
        },
        {
         "type": "italic"
        }
       ],
       "text": "Example",
       "type": "text"
      },
      {
       "marks": [
        {
         "attrs": {
          "color": "rgb(33, 37, 41)"
         },
         "type": "textStyle"
        }
       ],
       "text": ": Unique states of Go",
       "type": "text"
      }
     ],
     "type": "paragraph"
    },
    {
     "attrs": {
      "textAlign": "justify"
     },
     "content": [
      {
       "marks": [
        {
         "attrs": {
          "color": "rgb(33, 37, 41)"
         },
         "type": "textStyle"
        }
       ],
       "text": "A Go board has 19 × 19 points where a user can place a stone. Each of the points can be empty or occupied by black or white stone. By the Step Rule of Counting, we can compute the number of unique board configurations.",
       "type": "text"
      },
      {
       "text": " ",
       "type": "text"
      }
     ],
     "type": "paragraph"
    },
    {
     "attrs": {
      "align": "center",
      "id": "dcc0d47c-e49f-4df6-8c7c-023149e1d434",
      "naturalHeight": "1367",
      "naturalWidth": "2050",
      "src": "https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/probabilityforcs.firebasestorage.app/o/uploads%2Fimages%2Fdcc0d47c-e49f-4df6-8c7c-023149e1d434?alt=media&token=dce990f6-cb4b-4a68-9585-61a3229b1d1f",
      "widthPct": "80"
     },
     "type": "kindImage"
    },
    {
     "attrs": {
      "textAlign": "justify"
     },
     "content": [
      {
       "marks": [
        {
         "type": "italic"
        }
       ],
       "text": "In Go there are 19x19 points. Each point can have a black stone, white stone, or no stone at all.",
       "type": "text"
      }
     ],
     "type": "paragraph"
    },
    {
     "attrs": {
      "textAlign": "justify"
     },
     "content": [
      {
       "text": "Here we are going to construct the board one point at a time, step by step. Each time we add a point we have a unique choice where we can decide to make the point one of three options: {Black, White, No Stone}. Using this construction we can apply the Step Rule of Counting. If there was only one point, there would be three unique board configurations. If there were four points you would have 3⋅3⋅3⋅",
       "type": "text"
      },
      {
       "attrs": {
        "showEditor": false,
        "value": "3=81"
       },
       "type": "math_inline"
      },
      {
       "text": " unique combinations. In Go there are ",
       "type": "text"
      },
      {
       "attrs": {
        "showEditor": false,
        "value": "3^{(19×19)} \\approx 10^{172}"
       },
       "type": "math_inline"
      },
      {
       "text": " possible board positions. The way we constructed our board didn't take into account which ones were illegal by the rules of Go. It turns out that \"only\" about ",
       "type": "text"
      },
      {
       "attrs": {
        "showEditor": false,
        "value": "10^{170}"
       },
       "type": "math_inline"
      },
      {
       "text": " of those positions are legal. That is about the square of the number of atoms in the universe. In other words: to match the number of Go board configurations, you’d need one universe of atoms for each atom in our universe.",
       "type": "text"
      }
     ],
     "type": "paragraph"
    },
    {
     "attrs": {
      "textAlign": "justify"
     },
     "content": [
      {
       "text": "As a computer scientist this sort of result can be very important. While computers are powerful, an algorithm which needed to store each configuration of the board would not be a reasonable approach. No computer can store more information than atoms in the universe squared!",
       "type": "text"
      }
     ],
     "type": "paragraph"
    }
   ],
   "type": "purpleBox"
  },
  {
   "attrs": {
    "id": "counting-with-or",
    "level": "2",
    "textAlign": "justify"
   },
   "content": [
    {
     "text": "Counting with Or",
     "type": "text"
    }
   ],
   "type": "heading"
  },
  {
   "attrs": {
    "textAlign": "justify"
   },
   "content": [
    {
     "marks": [
      {
       "attrs": {
        "color": "rgb(33, 37, 41)"
       },
       "type": "textStyle"
      }
     ],
     "text": "If you want to consider the total number of unique outcomes, when outcomes can come from two or more sources (for example source ",
     "type": "text"
    },
    {
     "attrs": {
      "showEditor": false,
      "value": "A"
     },
     "type": "math_inline"
    },
    {
     "marks": [
      {
       "attrs": {
        "color": "rgb(33, 37, 41)"
       },
       "type": "textStyle"
      }
     ],
     "text": " ",
     "type": "text"
    },
    {
     "marks": [
      {
       "type": "bold"
      }
     ],
     "text": "or",
     "type": "text"
    },
    {
     "marks": [
      {
       "attrs": {
        "color": "rgb(33, 37, 41)"
       },
       "type": "textStyle"
      }
     ],
     "text": " source ",
     "type": "text"
    },
    {
     "attrs": {
      "showEditor": false,
      "value": "B"
     },
     "type": "math_inline"
    },
    {
     "text": ")",
     "type": "text"
    },
    {
     "marks": [
      {
       "attrs": {
        "color": "rgb(33, 37, 41)"
       },
       "type": "textStyle"
      }
     ],
     "text": ", then the equation you use depends on whether or not there exists outcomes which are in ",
     "type": "text"
    },
    {
     "marks": [
      {
       "attrs": {
        "color": "rgb(33, 37, 41)"
       },
       "type": "textStyle"
      },
      {
       "type": "bold"
      }
     ],
     "text": "both",
     "type": "text"
    },
    {
     "marks": [
      {
       "attrs": {
        "color": "rgb(33, 37, 41)"
       },
       "type": "textStyle"
      }
     ],
     "text": " ",
     "type": "text"
    },
    {
     "attrs": {
      "showEditor": false,
      "value": "A"
     },
     "type": "math_inline"
    },
    {
     "marks": [
      {
       "attrs": {
        "color": "rgb(33, 37, 41)"
       },
       "type": "textStyle"
      }
     ],
     "text": " and ",
     "type": "text"
    },
    {
     "attrs": {
      "showEditor": false,
      "value": "B"
     },
     "type": "math_inline"
    },
    {
     "marks": [
      {
       "attrs": {
        "color": "rgb(33, 37, 41)"
       },
       "type": "textStyle"
      }
     ],
     "text": ". If not, you can use the simpler \"Mutually Exclusive Counting\" rule. Otherwise you need to use the slightly more involved Inclusion Exclusion rule. Formally, the ",
     "type": "text"
    },
    {
     "marks": [
      {
       "attrs": {
        "color": "rgb(33, 37, 41)"
       },
       "type": "textStyle"
      },
      {
       "type": "bold"
      }
     ],
     "text": "or ",
     "type": "text"
    },
    {
     "marks": [
      {
       "attrs": {
        "color": "rgb(33, 37, 41)"
       },
       "type": "textStyle"
      }
     ],
     "text": "of two sets is called ",
     "type": "text"
    },
    {
     "marks": [
      {
       "attrs": {
        "class": null,
        "href": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/union_(set_theory)#:~:text=in%20set%20theory%2c%20the%20union,equal%20to%20the%20empty%20set.",
        "rel": "noopener noreferrer nofollow",
        "target": "_blank"
       },
       "type": "link"
      }
     ],
     "text": "the union",
     "type": "text"
    },
    {
     "marks": [
      {
       "attrs": {
        "color": "rgb(33, 37, 41)"
       },
       "type": "textStyle"
      }
     ],
     "text": ".",
     "type": "text"
    }
   ],
   "type": "paragraph"
  },
  {
   "attrs": {
    "id": null,
    "level": "3",
    "textAlign": "justify"
   },
   "content": [
    {
     "text": "Counting with Or, the Mutually Exclusive Case",
     "type": "text"
    }
   ],
   "type": "heading"
  },
  {
   "content": [
    {
     "attrs": {
      "textAlign": "justify"
     },
     "content": [
      {
       "marks": [
        {
         "type": "bold"
        },
        {
         "type": "italic"
        }
       ],
       "text": "Definition",
       "type": "text"
      },
      {
       "text": ": Counting the \"or\" of two sets, Mutually Exclusive case.",
       "type": "text"
      }
     ],
     "type": "paragraph"
    },
    {
     "attrs": {
      "textAlign": "justify"
     },
     "content": [
      {
       "text": "If the outcome of an experiment can either be drawn from set ",
       "type": "text"
      },
      {
       "attrs": {
        "showEditor": false,
        "value": "A"
       },
       "type": "math_inline"
      },
      {
       "text": " or set ",
       "type": "text"
      },
      {
       "attrs": {
        "showEditor": false,
        "value": "B"
       },
       "type": "math_inline"
      },
      {
       "text": ", where none of the outcomes in set ",
       "type": "text"
      },
      {
       "attrs": {
        "showEditor": false,
        "value": "A"
       },
       "type": "math_inline"
      },
      {
       "text": " are the same as any of the outcomes in set ",
       "type": "text"
      },
      {
       "attrs": {
        "showEditor": false,
        "value": "B"
       },
       "type": "math_inline"
      },
      {
       "text": " (called mutual exclusion), then:",
       "type": "text"
      }
     ],
     "type": "paragraph"
    },
    {
     "attrs": {
      "rawTex": "\\begin{aligned}\n&\\text{Number of outcomes}\\\\\n&=|A \\or B|\\\\\n&=|A|+|B|\n\\end{aligned}"
     },
     "type": "block-tex"
    }
   ],
   "type": "borderedBox"
  },
  {
   "content": [
    {
     "attrs": {
      "textAlign": "justify"
     },
     "content": [
      {
       "marks": [
        {
         "type": "bold"
        }
       ],
       "text": "Example: ",
       "type": "text"
      },
      {
       "marks": [
        {
         "attrs": {
          "color": "rgb(33, 37, 41)"
         },
         "type": "textStyle"
        }
       ],
       "text": "A route finding algorithm needs to find routes from Nairobi to Dar Es Salaam. It finds routes that either pass through Mt Kilimanjaro or Mombasa. There are 20 routes that pass through Mt Kilimanjaro, 15 routes that pass through Mombasa and 0 routes which pass through both Mt Kilimanjaro and Mombasa. How many routes are there total?",
       "type": "text"
      }
     ],
     "type": "paragraph"
    },
    {
     "attrs": {
      "textAlign": "justify"
     },
     "content": [
      {
       "marks": [
        {
         "attrs": {
          "color": "rgb(33, 37, 41)"
         },
         "type": "textStyle"
        },
        {
         "type": "bold"
        }
       ],
       "text": "Solution: ",
       "type": "text"
      },
      {
       "marks": [
        {
         "attrs": {
          "color": "rgb(33, 37, 41)"
         },
         "type": "textStyle"
        }
       ],
       "text": "Routes can come from either Mt Kilimanjaro ",
       "type": "text"
      },
      {
       "marks": [
        {
         "type": "bold"
        }
       ],
       "text": "or",
       "type": "text"
      },
      {
       "marks": [
        {
         "attrs": {
          "color": "rgb(33, 37, 41)"
         },
         "type": "textStyle"
        }
       ],
       "text": " Mombasa. The two sets of routes are mutually exclusive as there are zero routes which are in both groups. As such the total number of routes is addition: 20 + 15 = 35.",
       "type": "text"
      }
     ],
     "type": "paragraph"
    }
   ],
   "type": "purpleBox"
  },
  {
   "attrs": {
    "textAlign": "justify"
   },
   "content": [
    {
     "text": "If you can show that two groups are mutually exclusive counting becomes simple addition. Of course not all sets are mutually exclusive. In the example above, imagine there had been a single route which went through both Mt Kilimanjaro and Mombasa. We would have double counted that route because it would be included in both the sets. If sets are not mutually exclusive, counting the ",
     "type": "text"
    },
    {
     "marks": [
      {
       "type": "bold"
      }
     ],
     "text": "or",
     "type": "text"
    },
    {
     "text": " is still addition, we simply need to take into account any double counting.",
     "type": "text"
    }
   ],
   "type": "paragraph"
  },
  {
   "attrs": {
    "textAlign": "justify"
   },
   "content": [
    {
     "text": "If you have more than two sets, and they are all mutually exclusive (no one outcome is in more than one set) then this rule will generalize:",
     "type": "text"
    }
   ],
   "type": "paragraph"
  },
  {
   "content": [
    {
     "attrs": {
      "textAlign": "justify"
     },
     "content": [
      {
       "marks": [
        {
         "type": "bold"
        },
        {
         "type": "italic"
        }
       ],
       "text": "Definition",
       "type": "text"
      },
      {
       "text": ": Counting the \"or\" of many sets, Mutually Exclusive case.",
       "type": "text"
      }
     ],
     "type": "paragraph"
    },
    {
     "attrs": {
      "textAlign": "justify"
     },
     "content": [
      {
       "text": "If the outcome of an experiment can come from set ",
       "type": "text"
      },
      {
       "attrs": {
        "showEditor": false,
        "value": "S_1"
       },
       "type": "math_inline"
      },
      {
       "text": " or ",
       "type": "text"
      },
      {
       "attrs": {
        "showEditor": false,
        "value": "S_2"
       },
       "type": "math_inline"
      },
      {
       "text": " or ",
       "type": "text"
      },
      {
       "attrs": {
        "showEditor": false,
        "value": "\\cdots"
       },
       "type": "math_inline"
      },
      {
       "text": " or ",
       "type": "text"
      },
      {
       "attrs": {
        "showEditor": false,
        "value": "S_n"
       },
       "type": "math_inline"
      },
      {
       "text": " and there is no outcome which is in more than one set (in other words, the sets are mutually exclusive), then:",
       "type": "text"
      }
     ],
     "type": "paragraph"
    },
    {
     "attrs": {
      "rawTex": "\\begin{aligned}\n&\\text{Number of outcomes}\\\\\n&=|S_1 \\or S_2 \\or \\cdots \\or S_n|\\\\\n&=\\sum_{i=1}^n |S_i|\n\\end{aligned}"
     },
     "type": "block-tex"
    }
   ],
   "type": "borderedBox"
  },
  {
   "attrs": {
    "id": "counting-with-or-the-general-case",
    "level": "3",
    "textAlign": "justify"
   },
   "content": [
    {
     "text": "Counting with Or, the General Case",
     "type": "text"
    }
   ],
   "type": "heading"
  },
  {
   "attrs": {
    "textAlign": "justify"
   },
   "content": [
    {
     "text": "If you want to count using the \"or\" of different sets of outcomes, but the sets are not mutually exclusive, you will need to use the more complicated general case which is called Inclusion-Exclusion:",
     "type": "text"
    }
   ],
   "type": "paragraph"
  },
  {
   "content": [
    {
     "attrs": {
      "textAlign": "justify"
     },
     "content": [
      {
       "marks": [
        {
         "type": "bold"
        },
        {
         "type": "italic"
        }
       ],
       "text": "Definition",
       "type": "text"
      },
      {
       "text": ": Counting the \"or\" of two sets, Inclusion-Exclusion Case",
       "type": "text"
      }
     ],
     "type": "paragraph"
    },
    {
     "attrs": {
      "textAlign": "justify"
     },
     "content": [
      {
       "text": "If the outcome of an experiment can either be drawn from set ",
       "type": "text"
      },
      {
       "attrs": {
        "showEditor": false,
        "value": "A"
       },
       "type": "math_inline"
      },
      {
       "text": " or set ",
       "type": "text"
      },
      {
       "attrs": {
        "showEditor": false,
        "value": "B"
       },
       "type": "math_inline"
      },
      {
       "text": ", and sets ",
       "type": "text"
      },
      {
       "attrs": {
        "showEditor": false,
        "value": "A"
       },
       "type": "math_inline"
      },
      {
       "text": " and ",
       "type": "text"
      },
      {
       "attrs": {
        "showEditor": false,
        "value": "B"
       },
       "type": "math_inline"
      },
      {
       "text": " may potentially overlap (i.e., it is not the case that ",
       "type": "text"
      },
      {
       "attrs": {
        "showEditor": false,
        "value": "A"
       },
       "type": "math_inline"
      },
      {
       "text": " and ",
       "type": "text"
      },
      {
       "attrs": {
        "showEditor": false,
        "value": "B"
       },
       "type": "math_inline"
      },
      {
       "text": " are mutually exclusive), then the number of outcomes of the experiment is:",
       "type": "text"
      }
     ],
     "type": "paragraph"
    },
    {
     "attrs": {
      "rawTex": "\\begin{aligned}\n&\\text{Number of outcomes} \\\\\n&= |A \\or B|\\\\\n&=|A|+|B|−|A \\and B|\n\\end{aligned}"
     },
     "type": "block-tex"
    }
   ],
   "type": "borderedBox"
  },
  {
   "attrs": {
    "textAlign": "justify"
   },
   "content": [
    {
     "text": "If you have more than two sets, and they are not mutually exclusive, the equation gets substantially more complex. If there are three sets, the inclusion exclusion rule is:",
     "type": "text"
    }
   ],
   "type": "paragraph"
  },
  {
   "attrs": {
    "sourceBytes": 3417,
    "sourceSha256": "8eba86aefe34e7789e2bc635e07ad5308c61ac0c35623a96965cfad32b087cb1"
   },
   "type": "interactive-demo"
  },
  {
   "attrs": {
    "textAlign": "justify"
   },
   "content": [
    {
     "text": "To calculate the size of union (aka counting with ",
     "type": "text"
    },
    {
     "marks": [
      {
       "type": "bold"
      }
     ],
     "text": "or",
     "type": "text"
    },
    {
     "text": "), of three sets you: (1) add the size of each set on their own. (2) Then you need to subtract off the size of the intersection (aka the ",
     "type": "text"
    },
    {
     "marks": [
      {
       "type": "bold"
      }
     ],
     "text": "and",
     "type": "text"
    },
    {
     "text": ") of every possible ",
     "type": "text"
    },
    {
     "marks": [
      {
       "type": "italic"
      }
     ],
     "text": "pair",
     "type": "text"
    },
    {
     "text": " of sets. (3) Finally, you add in the count of elements in all three sets. ",
     "type": "text"
    }
   ],
   "type": "paragraph"
  },
  {
   "attrs": {
    "textAlign": "justify"
   },
   "content": [
    {
     "text": "If there are more than three sets the general rule of Inclusion-Exclusion gets even more complex. If you change the number of sets in the equation above you can explore how the formula expands. As a rule of thumb, if you find yourself needing to use this Inclusion-Exclusion rule when you have more than three sets, it might be a sign that you want to look for another approach to solve your problem. ",
     "type": "text"
    }
   ],
   "type": "paragraph"
  }
 ],
 "type": "doc"
}
